course: religion-ethics
lesson: 95
title: Honesty with money: too much change and the found wallet
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Money that comes to you by mistake, or that you find, is not yours just because you have it: it still belongs to someone.
explain: The shopkeeper gives Ruby £5 too much change and her mate says, "Result! Keep it"; but in many shops the cashier has to make up the missing money, so it may come out of a real person's pay.
explain: "Finders keepers" is a playground saying, not a rule: the Bible says to give back what your neighbour has lost (Deuteronomy 22:1-3), and in many places found money should be handed in; if nobody claims it after a while, it is often given back to the finder.
explain: Honesty with money also means not hiding problems when you sell something, not taking from people who can't notice, and using money for what it was given for.
explain: A famous story says young Abraham Lincoln walked miles to return a few cents to a customer, which helped earn him the name "Honest Abe".
fact: "You shall not steal" (Exodus 20:15); "The Lord detests dishonest scales" (Proverbs 11:1).
fact: In many shops the cashier has to make up missing money, so too much change may come out of their own pay.
fact: "Do the same if you find your neighbour's donkey or cloak or anything else they have lost" (Deuteronomy 22:1-3); "Keep it until they come looking for it, and then give it back" (Deuteronomy 22:2).
fact: In a big 2019 study, researchers handed in 17,000 "lost" wallets in 40 countries, and more people returned wallets that had more money in them.
fact: In Judaism, returning lost property is a commandment.
fact: In Aesop's fable, the honest woodcutter said only the iron axe was his and was given all three; a greedy neighbour claimed the golden one and lost his own; fables are invented stories with a lesson.
fact: A machine has an owner, so keeping a machine's mistake profits from someone's loss.
fact: Money paid into your account by mistake is not yours; banks will ask for it back, and the honest thing is to tell them.
fact: Whoever finds lost property and lies about it must return it and add a fifth (Leviticus 6:2-5).
fact: "Whoever can be trusted with very little can also be trusted with much" (Luke 16:10-11): using money for what it was given for builds trust.
fact: If Gran says "keep the change", telling her how much is left makes it an honest gift; being open about money stops mix-ups in families.
fact: Taking from someone who can't notice, like a little child, is still taking, and many think it's worse because they trust you.
fact: "Do not put a stumbling block in front of the blind" (Leviticus 19:14): when you sell something, tell the buyer about problems that affect their safety.
fact: Money given at church is held in trust for the people it helps, so churches count it with two people; Ananias and Sapphira's story shows how seriously the first church took money given to God (Acts 5:1-11).
fact: Slang check: "result!" means great luck; "minted" means rich; "a fiver" is a £5 note; "pet" (North East England) means love; "dodgy" means not working properly.
exercise: The shopkeeper gives Ruby £5 too much change. Her mate says, "Result! Keep it." What should Ruby do? | Give it back; it may come out of the cashier's own pay | Keep it, the shop made the mistake, so it's their own fault | Spend it quickly before anyone notices | Give half back and keep the rest
exercise: Tom finds a wallet with £50 and a bank card in the park. What is right? | Hand it in or contact the owner with a grown-up's help | Keep the money and post the card back | Keep it all, because finders keepers is the rule everywhere | Leave it there for someone else to find
exercise: What did the big 2019 wallet study find? | More people returned wallets with more money in them | Nobody returned any wallets at all | Only wallets with no money came back | People kept every single wallet that had any money in it
exercise: In Aesop's fable, which axe did the honest woodcutter say was his? | The iron one | The golden one | The silver one | All three of them
exercise: Kai says, "If he'd said gold he'd be minted." What does "minted" mean? | Rich | Happy | Lucky | Clever
exercise: A vending machine drops two cans for Lucas when he paid for one. What should he do? | Tell the staff or the owner; it's their can | Keep it, because machines can't lose anything | Drink it fast so nobody sees | Hit the machine to get a third
exercise: Mia finds a $20 note on the footpath in Sydney. "Finders keepers," says her brother. What is best? | Hand it in with a grown-up; if nobody claims it, it may become hers | Keep it, because finders keepers is the law | Leave it on the ground for someone else | Give it to the first person who walks by
exercise: Dad's bank puts £100 into his account by mistake. What should he do? | Tell the bank; it isn't his | Spend it quickly before they notice | Keep it, because the bank made the mistake | Move it to another bank account
exercise: Josh was given lunch money, ate free fruit and spent it on sweets. What is honest now? | Tell Mum and pay it back from his pocket money | Say the dinner lady lost his money, so it's not his fault | Buy Mum some sweets to share, so it all evens out | Do it again tomorrow, but this time buy some fruit
exercise: Gran says, "Keep the change, pet," for a fiver of milk. Should Ellie still say how much is left? | Yes; telling Gran the amount makes it an honest gift | No; Gran said to keep it, so it really doesn't matter at all | No; it's rude to talk about money with Gran | Yes, and then ask Gran for more
exercise: Amira is tempted to swap Ted's tooth-fairy £2 for 20p; he can't read money yet. Is it stealing? | Yes; taking from someone who won't notice is still stealing | No, because Ted doesn't know the difference between the coins yet | No, because it's only tooth-fairy money | Only if Ted cries about it
exercise: Harry sells his bike online and doesn't mention the dodgy brakes. What should he do? | Tell the buyer about the brakes; their safety depends on it | Say nothing; buyers should always check everything for themselves | Lower the price a little but say nothing | Paint the brakes so they look new
exercise: A coin from the collection plate rolls under a pew. Whose is it? | It belongs to the people the collection helps | It belongs to Liam, because he was the one who found it | It belongs to the person sitting nearest to where it stopped | It belongs to nobody now, so anyone can keep it
exercise: What does Deuteronomy 22:2 say about lost things? | Keep it until they come looking, then give it back | Keep it if nobody saw you find it, because then it's yours | Give it to the poorest person you know | Throw it away, because lost things are unlucky
exercise: What was Abraham Lincoln's nickname from the story of returning a few cents? | Honest Abe | Clever Abe | Lucky Abe | Rich Abe
test: Your mate says, "Shops are rich, the change doesn't matter." What is the best reply? | The cashier might have to pay it back from their own pay | He's right, so keep any change you get | Shops give extra change on purpose | It only matters if the change is over £100, otherwise keep it
test: "Finders keepers, losers weepers, mate," says Mia's brother. What is the truth about that saying? | It's a playground saying, not a rule; found money should be handed in | It's the law in every country in the world | It's from the Bible, so it must be true | It only works for coins, not notes
test: A cartoon shows a woodcutter who claims the gold axe and gets all three. What is wrong? | In the fable the honest woodcutter got all three; the greedy liar lost his own | Nothing; that is how Aesop told it | The woodcutter dropped a silver axe | There were four axes in the fable
test: What would happen if everyone kept wallets they found? | People who lose things would never get them back, and trust would fall | Everyone would be richer and happier | Nobody would ever lose a wallet again | Wallets would stop being made
test: "Bloody machine's always eating my money, so this can's mine," says Lucas. What is the honest view? | If he lost money before, tell the owner; keeping their can isn't the fix | He's right, so he can keep both cans | Machines owe everyone free cans | Kick the machine until it gives his money back
test: Dad says, "The bank won't notice £100." What is accurate? | Banks will ask for it back; it isn't his | Banks never notice small mistakes like that | It becomes his to keep if nobody asks within a week | Banks give free money as a reward to good customers
test: Amira's friend says, "Ted's little, he'll never know about the £2." Why is that worse, not better? | Ted trusts her, and taking from someone who can't notice is still stealing | Because Ted might learn to read money soon | It isn't worse; little kids don't need money | Because the tooth fairy will find out
test: Harry says, "It's not a lie, I just didn't mention the brakes." What is the problem? | Leaving out a danger misleads the buyer and could get them hurt | There's no problem; you can only lie with words, not by keeping quiet | Brakes are the buyer's job to check | It's fine because the bike was cheap
test: Liam's friend whispers, "Keep the coin, nobody saw, it's only 50p." What should Liam do? | Put it back in the plate; it belongs to the people it helps | Keep it, because it rolled away from the plate, so it's lost now | Spend it on sweets after church | Give it to his friend instead
test: Ellie's brother says, "Just keep the change and say nothing to Gran." Why tell Gran anyway? | Being open about money stops misunderstandings in families | Because Gran might want the change back for her bus fare later | Because it is rude to keep any money | Because Gran is checking the receipt
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the obligation arising upon the receipt of excessive change? | The excess ought to be returned, it being the property of another | The excess may be retained, the error being that of the shopkeeper | The excess may be retained provided that it is spent promptly | The excess ought to be donated to a charity of one's choosing
exam: Select the statement consistent with Deuteronomy 22:1-3. | Property lost by a neighbour ought to be kept safely and restored to the owner | Property lost by a neighbour becomes the possession of the finder | Lost property ought to be destroyed to avoid disputes | Lost property may be retained if the owner is unknown to the finder
exam: Identify the principal finding of the 2019 study in which some 17,000 wallets were handed in across 40 countries. | Wallets containing more money were returned more frequently | Wallets containing money were never returned | Wallets were returned only in a single country | The amount of money had no relation whatsoever to the outcome
exam: Given that a person sells a bicycle with defective brakes, ascertain the conduct most consistent with this lesson. | The disclosure of the defect to the purchaser, whose safety depends upon it | The concealment of the defect, the purchaser being responsible for inspection | The reduction of the price without disclosure of the defect | The cosmetic repair of the brakes so as to conceal the defect
